{
  "dom_id": "step-q104037",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200385/assets/6d9d6bbd1f54319a6bf1aa8dae03d6ea7dc19e8430e7a5bfd72e805edbcfb6d5.png)\n\nThe graph $y={g}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $y=g(x)$, is shown above. Which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\nI. The graph of $y=g(x)$ is concave down on the interval $(-1,1)$\n\nII. $g(x)$ has a critical point at $x=-2$\n\nIII. $g(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=1$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's examine each of the statements separately.\n\n- Statement I is false. The graph ${g}^{′}(x)$ is increasing on the interval $(0,1).$ Hence, ${g}^{″}(x)$ must be positive on $(0,1)$ and therefore, the graph of $y=g(x)$ is concave up on $(0,1)⊂(-1,1).$\n\n- Statement II is false. From the graph, we can conclude that ${g}^{′}(-2)$ exists and that it is not equal to $0.$ So, $x=-2$ is not a critical point of the function.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the first derivative ${g}^{′}(x)$ changes its sign at the point $x=1,$ from negative to positive. Therefore $g(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\".",
